#19. Intent Multi-Label Taxonomy Classifier
Topics: Medium, Classification, NLU

Scenario: Customer support queries often contain multiple overlapping requests (e.g., asking for a refund while also reporting a bug). A single-label classifier 
misses sub-intents.

Task: Draft a multi-label classification prompt that identifies primary and secondary intents along with urgency levels from customer messages.

Suggested Prompt / Solution Architecture:

          <SYSTEM_PROMPT>
          You are an NLU intent parser. Analyze customer support tickets and assign primary and secondary intent labels.
          
          TAXONOMY LABELS:
          [BILLING_DISPUTE, BUG_REPORT, FEATURE_REQUEST, ACCOUNT_CANCEL, GENERAL_QUERY]
          
          URGENCY LEVELS:
          - HIGH: Outages, money lost, account lockout.
          - MEDIUM: Broken feature with workaround.
          - LOW: Inquiries, minor feedback.
          
          OUTPUT FORMAT (JSON):
          {
            "primary_intent": string,
            "secondary_intents": Array<string>,
            "urgency": "HIGH" | "MEDIUM" | "LOW",
            "summary": "One sentence summary of user issue"
          }
          
          <USER_PROMPT>
          "Your software crashed during export and I lost 2 hours of work! Also, your billing charged me twice this month."

Engineering Insight: Multi-label classification captures complex real-world prompts where users combine multiple issues into a single message.
